A. Relevance of an Automation Human-Machine Interface
In order to support the driver in safely handling malfunctions during partially automated driving, it is essential to provide adequate information to the driver via a so-called automation Human-Machine Interface (HMI) [10]. Here, information about the system status and the maneuvers of the AV is considered important [11]–[13]. In addition, information about maneuver reasons, environmental information, speed and speed limits also seems to be important in partially automated driving [14]. This has also been conﬁrmed for urban automated driving [15]. The design of the HMI is challenging; the driver should be adequately informed by the HMI, but at the same time the driver should not be visually distracted in the monitoring task of the driving environment [16], since more than 90% of information is perceived visually while driving [17]. It has been observed that partially automated driving leads to reduced visual attention and longer off-road

