B. Potential of Augmented Reality Head-Up Displays
Augmented Reality Head-Up Displays (ARHUDs) represent an innovative approach to the design of the HMI. The ﬁrst investigations in the automotive context were already carried out in the 1970s [27], but are now experiencing an increase in relevance due to their technical feasibility. An ARHUD is characterized by displaying virtual objects and information in the driving environment in real time [28]. This allows the information to be displayed at the actual position and thus virtual objects can overlay the environment from the drivers’ perspective [29]. ARHUD can thus direct the drivers’ attention via the virtual objects to allow them to react faster [30] without having to take their eyes off the road. Therefore, ARHUDs may lead to higher safety and comfort [31]. Previous research in the context of automated driving has so far shown positive subjective results [32]–[36] and higher visual attention to the driving environment when using an ARHUD [32], [37]. There is also a positive trend for the ARHUD in regard to take-over performance, but the results depend on the scenarios and the design of the ARHUD and are less clear [37], [38]. A pre-study [32] showed decreased subjective workload and increased trust of an ARHUD concept compared to a conventional head-up display (HUD) concept for monitoring in partially automated urban driving. Additionally, the ARHUD resulted in higher visual attention to the driving environment [32]. In regard to the monitoring of functionalities of an adaptive cruise control, drivers reported a higher level of safety and a lower level of workload and distraction when using an ARHUD compared to an instrument cluster [33]. Showing drivers an augmented visualization of the vehicle’s sensor data also improved acceptance and trust in the AV [34]. In a further study [35], participants reported that they would prefer an ARHUD compared to an instrument cluster in

conditionally automated driving. They considered the ARHUD more useful and easier to understand [35]. It could also be demonstrated that ARHUDs can improve situation awareness in automated driving [36]. Regarding automation failures in automated driving, an ARHUD led to shorter reaction times of the driver [38]. In contrast, another study [37] showed no differences in take-over time or crash rate when comparing an ARHUD to an instrument cluster. However, it found a higher visual attention ratio to the driving environment [37].
II. OBJECTIVES
In order to comply with the challenges of partially automated driving in complex urban areas from a human-centered perspective, an integrated HMI seems to be of great importance. With regard to malfunctions in partially automated driving, ARHUDs could have a high potential. It has not yet been investigated if an ARHUD actually provides better support for the driver in case of malfunctions during partially automated driving in urban areas. For this purpose, an ARHUD concept has been developed, which should be compared to a Baseline concept consisting of only an instrument cluster and an LED strip (see Section III). In a driving simulation experiment, the two concepts were evaluated regarding their effects on take-over time, take-over quality, visual attention and subjective ratings. This was to investigate the following research questions (RQs). Due to the positive subjective results of ARHUDs in previous research (see Section I. B.), we expected the ARHUD to result in higher subjective ratings than the Baseline concept.
RQ1: Does take-over time and quality differ depending on the HMI concept?
RQ2: Are there differences in take-over time and quality between the lateral and longitudinal malfunction?
RQ3: Does visual attention differ depending on the HMI concept?
RQ4: Does the ARHUD concept lead to higher subjective ratings compared to the Baseline concept?
On an explorative level, we wanted to additionally investigate whether safety and controllability can be ensured for the investigated malfunction scenarios.
III. HMI DESIGN
A. Baseline Concept
The Baseline concept consisted of a freely programmable instrument cluster and a LED strip mounted at the bottom of the windshield. The instrument cluster design was based on two previously conducted studies [39], [40]. It displayed several driving- and automation-related information items such as the system status of the ADS, current speed, speed limits, navigation, as well as actual and planned maneuvers. Since the system status was considered the most relevant information for automated driving [14], [15], this information was additionally displayed via an LED strip. The LED strip is particularly beneﬁcial due to its good peripheral perceptibility [10] and has already been used in several studies on automated driving (e.g., [15], [39], [41]–[43]). The active ADS was indicated by a blue illumination of the LED strip, since the color blue

