B. Augmented Reality Head-Up Display Concept
1) Requirements Deﬁnition: Whereas there are already guidelines for the design of the instrument cluster [46], [47] and recommendations for the design of the HMI for automated driving [48], [49], there are still no design principles for the ARHUD [50], [51]. We therefore collected quantitative and qualitative requirements resulting from ARHUD studies or transferred from conventional HMI concepts to the ARHUD context (Table I and Table II). This should provide a basis for the development of the ARHUD in this paper.
2) Development: The ARHUD concept consisted of the Baseline concept, but was extended by an additional ARHUD (Fig. 2). The ARHUD concept was based on a pre-study [32]

and was further developed. We displayed the driving-relevant information regarding AV guidance in the ARHUD. In addition to the augmented reality elements, the current speed was continously displayed in a conventional HUD. If an augmented visualization of the navigation information was not yet possible, e.g., because the intersection was not completely visible yet, this information was additionally displayed situationspeciﬁc in a two-dimensional manner in the HUD [50]. When the ADS was active, a single boomerang or boomerang chain was displayed in front of the AV (Fig. 2). When the vehicle was in free driving mode or stopped and no explicit maneuver such as turning at a intersection or a lane change was present, a single boomerang was displayed in front of the vehicle to reduce the risk of concealing other objects [50] (Fig. 2.a). The boomerang had the width of the AV, since this is the basis for estimating the lateral deviation of the AV [52]. The boomerang was displayed at a distance of 12 m in front of the AV, which was about 0.9 seconds away at 50 km/h and thus at the lower limit of the driver’s ﬁxations [53], [54]. The speed of 50 km/h was relevant because of the speed of 50 km/h set during the malfunction scenarios. In case of a lane change or a turning maneuver, the single boomerang was extended to a boomerang chain, which displayed the planned trajectory for an enhanced system understanding (Fig. 2.b and Fig. 2.c). Additionally, six more boomerang elements with a gap of 1 m in front of the single boomerang were shown. When following a vehicle in

