IV. METHOD
A. Sample
The participants were recruited via postings at the Technical University of Munich. The prerequisite for participation was the possession of a valid driver’s license for at least three years. Fifty-two participants took part in the driving simulator experiment. Due to simulator sickness, three participants had to abort the experiment. One participant had to be excluded because of nonfulﬁllment of the prerequisites for participation. This resulted in a total of 48 valid data sets. The mean age of the ARHUD group was M = 25.8 years (SD = 7.3) and the mean age of the Baseline group was M = 24.9 years (SD = 2.2). The ARHUD and the Baseline group each consisted of 10 (41.7%) women and 14 (58.3%) men. One participant in the Baseline and two participants in the ARHUD condition had a red-green color vision deﬁciency. When asked how regularly they drove in the past year, in the Baseline condition one participant reported daily, ﬁve participants reported several times a week, nine participants reported weekly, and nine participants reported monthly. In contrast, in the ARHUD condition, two participants reported driving daily, ﬁve participants reported driving several times a week, seven participants reported driving weekly, and 10 participants reported driving monthly. In the ARHUD group, 45.8% and in the Baseline group, 50.0% already had experience with a partially ADS. Both groups reported a high knowledge of automated driving (Mdn = 4.0) rated on a 5-point Likert scale from “very low (1)” to “very high (5)”.

