B. Experimental Design
We implemented a 2 × 2 mixed-subject design for the driving simulator study. The HMI concept (ARHUD or Baseline) was a between-subject factor and the malfunction scenario (longitudinal or lateral) was a within-subject factor. This was to avoid participants experiencing the same malfunction scenario multiple times. The malfunction scenarios were presented to the participants in permutated order. The experimental drive was interrupted after each take-over situation so that the participants did not have to drive for more than 10 minutes without a break. This should reduce vigilance decrements while the participants had to monitor the ADS [12], [57]. Participants were instructed that it was not necessary to keep their hands on the steering wheel when the ADS was active, since no reduced crash outcomes or improved driver conﬂict response were found when driving hands-on [4], [58], [59].

