C. Dependent Variables
We collected objective and subjective data during the experiment. We recorded take-over time, maximum lateral acceleration, minimum longitudinal acceleration and minimum time-to-collision (mTTC) [62] to evaluate take-over quality at system limits [63]. The crash rate was also recorded. Causing no crash was deﬁned as pass-criteria against which controllability can be assessed [64]. According to ISO 26262 standard [65], controllability is achieved if 90.0% of the participants fulﬁll this pass-criterion (class of controllability: C2). Visual attention was operationalized by the attention ratio on the areas of interest (AOIs) Road Ahead (corresponding to the windscreen) and Instrument Cluster, as well as offroad glances with a duration of more than 2 seconds [60]. Mirrors were not considered as further AOI, since these showed values of less than one percent in a pre-study [32] and should not be affected by the HMI concepts. Off-road glances greater than 2 seconds are considered to be an increased crash risk [60]. Using standardized questionnaires, we measured usability [66], subjective workload [67], [68], trust [69] and acceptance [70]. Additionally, participants rated the malfunction scenarios regarding the time budget (from 1 = “not sufﬁcient at all” to 5 = “completely sufﬁcient”), criticality (from 1 = “very critical” to 5 = “very uncritical”), complexity (from 1 = “very complex” to 5 = “very simple”) and predictability (from 1 = “very unpredictable” to 5 = “very predictable”) directly following the encountered malfunction scenario. These ratings were based on the factors classifying take-over scenarios [71]. The questionnaires were provided in German. Table III shows a list of the investigated dependent variables and their description.
D. Apparatus
We conducted the experiment in the static driving simulator of the Chair of Ergonomics at the Technical University of Munich [72] (Fig. 3). Three front projectors generated a 180◦ front view and three rear projectors generated the side and rear mirrors. Additionally, the front center projector realized the ARHUD visualization. The driving simulator software SILAB 6.0 of the Würzburg Institute for Trafﬁc Sciences [73] had a refresh rate of 60 Hz for visualization and a data collection rate of 240 Hz. A sound system created engine and environmental sounds. We recorded the eye-tracking data using the remote eye-tracking system SmartEye [74] using

three stationary infrared cameras in the vehicle interior running at 60 Hz. The ADS could be activated by pressing a button on the steering wheel. The ADS could be deactivated at any time by the participants by pressing the same button, by pressing the brake or accelerator pedal or by a steering intervention.
E. Experimental Track and Design of Malfunction Scenarios
The experimental track was a 27-minute automated drive, consisting of typical urban driving scenarios, such as following a vehicle in front, passing road bottlenecks or turning at intersections with trafﬁc lights. After 7 minutes of automated driving, a system limit scenario with a request to intervene occurred. The results of this scenario are not part of this publication. This was followed by two parts of 10 minutes automated driving followed by the lateral or longitudinal malfunction scenario depending on the permutation.
1) Longitudinal Malfunction Scenario: Before the malfunction occurred, the AV was on the right lane of a two-lane road and followed a van with a time gap of 2 seconds at a speed of 50 km/h (see Fig. 2.d). The van then changed to the left lane and a stopped vehicle became visible in the right lane (see Fig. 4.a). The ADS failed to recognize the new vehicle and drove towards the stopped vehicle without any request to intervene. The participants then had a time budget of 2.6 seconds to intervene in order to prevent a collision with the stopped vehicle. The scenario was based on cut-out scenarios in partially automated driving showing similar time budgets of 2.3-3 seconds on highways and rural roads [58], [59]. According to [71], we classiﬁed this scenario as having high urgency, low predictability, high criticality and high complexity of the necessary driver response. In the event of the malfunction, the ARHUD changed from ACC mode to free driving mode due to the missed detection of the stopped

