G. Statistical Analysis
Data preparation and visualization was done with Matlab and MS Excel. We used JASP 0.12 [77] for the statistical analysis. We checked the normality distribution and equality of variances by using the Shapiro–Wilk test and Levene’s test. If these assumptions for parametric tests were not met, we used the non-parametric alternative. We conducted an ANOVA independently of the assumption of normal distribution, since this seems to be robust against a violation of normal distribution [78]. Single items on Likert-scales were classiﬁed as ordinal data [79]. All other data were considered as interval scaled. A signiﬁcance level of α = 0.05 was applied. In case of multiple comparisons, a Bonferroni-Holm correction [80] was performed and the resulting αHolm was reported in the test statistics. We interpreted the effect sizes based on Cohen’s benchmark (small: d = 0.20, r = 0.10, partial η2 = 0.01; medium: d = 0.50, r = 0.30 partial η2 = 0.06; large: d = 0.80, r = 0.50 partial η2 = 0.14) [81]. Due to crashes or a lack of eye-tracking quality, which led to exclusions of participants regarding the take-over quality or visual attention, the sample sizes for all objective metrics are reported in the following.
