B. Eye-Tracking Data
The eye-tracking data of one participant was missing due to technical problems. Additionally, the eye-tracking data of two participants were excluded due to a data availability of less than 70.0% for both 10-minute automated drives before the malfunction [82]. This resulted in 23 valid datasets of the ARHUD group and 22 valid datasets of the Baseline group for the eye-tracking data. The remaining datasets had a mean data availability of 94.0% for the Baseline and of 92.9% for the ARHUD which is classiﬁed as good [82]. Fig. 6 shows the boxplots of the attention ratio on the AOIs Road Ahead and Instrument Cluster. The ARHUD resulted in a mean attention ratio of M = 89.9% (SD = 5.0%) on the Road Ahead and in an M = 1.7% (SD = 1.2%) on the Instrument Cluster. In comparison, the Baseline resulted in an M = 88.7% (SD = 3.3%) on the Road Ahead and in an M = 4.0% (SD = 2.6%) on the Instrument Cluster. The data of the attention ratio were not normally distributed and additionally, the assumption for equal variances were not fulﬁlled for the Instrument Cluster. There was no signiﬁcant difference for the Road Ahead (U = 321.0, p = 0.126, αHolm = 0.010). In contrast, the Instrument Cluster showed a signiﬁcant lower attention ratio for the ARHUD compared to the Baseline (U = 100.0, p < 0.001, r = 0.605, αHolm = 0.008).
Regarding the attention ratio during the malfunction scenarios, the data of three participants for the lateral malfunction and the data of two participants for the longitudinal malfunction had to be excluded because of a data availability of less than 70% [82]. In order to analyze the attention ratio during the malfunction scenarios, Mann-Whitney tests were performed because of the violation of the normality distribution (Table V).

