C. Subjective Data
The usability score (Fig. 7) had a mean of M = 66.04 (SD = 16.12) for the Baseline and a mean of M = 73.85 (SD = 14.01) for the ARHUD. An independent t-test revealed a signiﬁcantly higher usability for the ARHUD (t(46) = −1.792, p = 0.040, d = 0.517, one-tailed). The trust in automation score (Fig. 7) revealed a signiﬁcantly higher trust (t(46) = 1.876, p = 0.033, d = 0.542, one-tailed) for the ARHUD M = 3.00 (SD = 0.51) compared to the Baseline M = 2.70 (SD = 0.58). An exploratory analysis of trust scores in the ARHUD condition showed no reduction in trust with no crash (M = 2.84, SD = 0.41, n = 11) compared to one crash (M = 3.15, SD = 0.59, n = 11) and two crashes (M = 3.08, SD = 0.56, n = 2) at a descriptive level. The acceptance rating (Fig. 7) consisted of the usefulness and satisfaction score. The participants reported no signiﬁcantly higher satisfaction (t(46) = −1.942, p = 0.029, αHolm = 0.025, one-tailed) for the ARHUD (M = 0.93, SD = 0.69) compared to the Baseline

(M = 0.50, SD = 0.82). Due to the violation of the normality assumption, a conducted Mann-Whitney test (U = 237.5, p = 0.150, αHolm = 0.05, one-tailed) revealed no higher usefulness rating for the ARHUD (M = 0.89, SD = 0.61) than for the Baseline (M = 0.56, SD = 0.90). Table VI displays the means and standard deviations of the subjective workload for the longitudinal and lateral malfunction. The conducted ANOVA for the subjective workload showed neither a signiﬁcant effect for the HMI concept (F(1,46) = 2.252, p = 0.140), nor for the malfunction scenario (F(1,46) = 2.498, p = 0.121) or the interaction effect between the HMI concept and scenario (F(1,46) = 0.039, p = 0.845).
We compared the rating of the characteristics of the scenarios between the two different malfunction scenarios. The medians and the statistics are presented in Table VII. There were no signiﬁcant differences between the malfunction scenarios for the time budget, criticality and complexity. In contrast, there was a signiﬁcant higher rated predictability for the lateral malfunction scenario.
VI. DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS
A. Take-Over Time and Quality
The statistical analysis showed a signiﬁcantly shorter takeover time for ARHUD. Considering the take-over time for both malfunction scenarios separately, it seems that this is mainly due to the faster take-over time of the ARHUD during lateral malfunction. Even though there was only a difference of 0.21 seconds on the average take-over time between ARHUD and Baseline, we nevertheless assume that even this difference might be essential for the reduction of the crash rate. Emergency brake assistants initiate an intervention at a time budget of 0.90 seconds [6], since this is the last point in time at which the collision can be avoided by braking only. Therefore, we believe it makes a difference whether participants have a remaining average time budget of 0.88 seconds in the ARHUD compared 0.67 seconds in the Baseline condition

