to avoid a collision after they took over vehicle guidance in the lateral malfunction. On a descriptive level, both HMI concepts have a similar impact on the take-over time during longitudinal malfunction. The participants could detect that the AV is in danger of frontal collision with the stopped vehicle independently of the HMI concept. In this case, the ARHUD does not seem to provide faster intervention. In contrast, during lateral malfunction, the participants may be able to anticipate the trajectory of the AV better due to the more transparent visualization in the driving environment and thus to recognize the necessary intervention more quickly. This would be consistent with the literature stating that an ARHUD resulted in signiﬁcantly faster response time in the case of a lateral malfunction (lane change failure) [38], whereas no faster response time was detected in the case of a longitudinal malfunction (potential crash with front vehicle) [37]. Consequently, the scenario seems to have a considerable inﬂuence on the extent to which ARHUDs lead to a faster take-over time. On average, the ARHUD resulted in a take-over time of 2.8 seconds for the lateral malfunction with a time budget of 3.7 seconds, so that a time budget of 0.9 seconds remained to prevent the collision. These 0.9 seconds correspond to the moment when the displayed boomerang of the ARHUD reached the collision object, in this case the parked vehicle. Thus, there could be a great potential to display the single boomerang not 0.9 seconds in front of the vehicle but probably at the upper limit of 2 seconds [54]. There was a signiﬁcant difference in the take-over time between both malfunction scenarios. In the longitudinal malfunction scenario, participants took over the vehicle guidance signiﬁcantly faster.

This could be caused by the fact that the takeover time increases with an increasing time budget [63]. This contradicts that the participants perceived no difference in the time budget of the two malfunction scenarios according to the rating of the scenario characteristics. We assume that it is more likely to be due to the slow deviation of the middle of the lane becoming clearly visible at a later point in time and therefore resulting in longer take-over times during the lateral malfunction scenario.
When using the ARHUD, considerably fewer crashes occurred in both malfunction scenarios. This difference in the crash rate by the ARHUD is greater for the lateral malfunction than for the longitudinal malfunction. This could be due to the longer take-over times of the ARHUD during the lateral malfunction, since crashing drivers react slower than non-crashing drivers during the response process due to a malfunction [58]. Additionally, long braking reaction times lead to a higher crash probability [83]. In general, the investigated lateral malfunction seems to be less controllable than the longitudinal malfunction. The very high crash rate compared to previous studies [4], [5], [37] could be in consequence of the urban driving environment and the resulting high information density. An investigated urban malfunction during automated driving in a multi-vehicle simulation also showed a higher crash rate of 40% [39]. Thus, our investigated scenarios showed a higher time criticality compared to these studies. With regard to the other dependent variables of take-over quality, no differences were found between the HMI concepts. A comparison of the scenarios regarding these dependent variables was not possible. This was due to the small sample size, since participants with a collision were excluded from the

