analysis of the mTTC, minimum longitudinal acceleration and maximum lateral acceleration. Due to the reduced sample size, effects regarding the take-over quality might have not been detected. Although the ARHUD seems to provide signiﬁcant support in terms of take-over time and quality, no controllability according to ISO 26262 [65] was demonstrated for the investigated malfunction scenarios as a result of the high crash rate for either HMI concept. This demonstrates the need for a fallback level for partially automated urban driving. Although partially automated driving according to the SAE International J3016 [1] does not take into account non-sustained functions such as emergency braking [84], such functions may already contribute to a signiﬁcant crash reduction if the driver fails to intervene adequately. The number of vehicles equipped with an automatic emergency brake assistant is already rapidly increasing [85]. Nevertheless, it is important that the emergency brake assistant is a redundant system so that, for example, if a sensor failure occurs that leads to a malfunction of the partially ADS, this failure does not also affect the emergency brake assistant.
B. Visual Attention
There were no differences in the attention ratio on the Road Ahead between the two HMI concepts. Both HMI concepts resulted in high visual attention to the Road Ahead, so we assume that inattention should not have a strong effect on the take-over performance. With a high proportion of eyes on the road, the prerequisite for being able to visually detect malfunctions at all seems to be fulﬁlled. However, it is apparently not enough to just have eyes on the road to realize that the vehicle cannot handle the situation and an intervention is necessary [4]. Based on the results of the take-over time and quality, the ARHUD seems to better indicate the need for intervention to the driver. Another factor that could have a negative impact on take-over despite high visual attention would be mind-wandering. During mind-wandering, participants shift their mental attention to spend time with their own thoughts, which could lead to longer braking response times [86]. The high attention ratios to the Road Ahead compared to [18] could be due to experiencing two take-over situations with a request to intervene during the familiarization drive or differences in the instruction. With regard to the attention ratio on the Instrument Cluster, the Baseline concept showed a signiﬁcantly higher ratio. Since there was no difference in the number of off-road glances longer than 2 seconds between the two HMI concepts, it can be concluded that the higher attention ratio on the Instrument Cluster for the Baseline does not cause negative effects in the form of long off-road glances compared to the ARHUD. Nevertheless, even glances of normal duration can cause crashes if the timing is bad [87], e.g., during the occurrence of the malfunction. Therefore, the ARHUD seems to be beneﬁcial, since a lower attention ratio on the Instrument Cluster was necessary. The low absolute number of long off-road glances should nevertheless only be compared relatively between the two concepts. If participants were looking towards the vehicle interior and thus not on the AOIs Instrument Cluster or Road Ahead, the positioning of the three stationary eye-tracking cameras

may have led to non-available data and off-road glances were not recorded as such.
C. Subjective Ratings
The ARHUD led to a signiﬁcantly higher usability compared to the Baseline. The usability of the ARHUD can be classiﬁed as good and that of the Baseline as OK [88]. Consequently, the participants felt that the ARHUD was more effective, more efﬁcient and more satisfactory in terms of handling malfunctions and for monitoring in partially automated driving. With regard to the acceptance rating, there were no signiﬁcant differences between the two HMI concepts, so that it cannot be clearly concluded which of the systems the participants are more likely to use. However, the ARHUD seems to result in higher acceptance on a descriptive level. The ARHUD resulted in a signiﬁcantly higher trust in automation, which could conﬁrm the results of [32], [34]. In any case, a crash during a take-over could reduce the trust level in the AV [89]. However, since the descriptive analysis of the trust scores in the ARHUD condition showed no decrease in trust because of a crash, it can be assumed that the increased trust in the ARHUD condition indeed is due to the design of the ARHUD. Whereas the pre-study by [32] showed a signiﬁcantly lower workload when using an ARHUD to monitor the partially ADS, this could not be conﬁrmed for the investigated malfunction scenarios. There was no signiﬁcant difference in workload between the HMI concepts or between the scenarios. Reference [21] listed mean workload levels of automated driving studies, ranging from 10 to 42 for the NASA-TLX. Our results indicate higher workloads regardless of the HMI concept and scenario. From this, it could be concluded that the malfunctions were generally perceived as demanding in the context of automated driving. The ratings of the malfunction characteristics by the participants showed high criticality, relatively low predictability, medium complexity of the driver response and low to medium time budget. There were no signiﬁcant differences except for predictability, which was perceived less predictable for the longitudinal malfunction compared to the lateral malfunction. This could be because the longitudinal malfunction was only detectable from the moment the van changed to the left lane and the stopped vehicle became visible. In the case of the lateral malfunction, the participants might perceive slow drifting away from the middle of the lane as more predictive.
D. Further Limitations
One limitation is the young average age of the sample of this study. This could limit the generalizability of the results, since younger participants showed better results than older participants in monitoring and detecting failures of an automated system [90]. Older participants may show longer reaction times during the monitoring task [90], which in the context of this study could have a negative effect on the takeover time and quality. The participants of this study were not used to partially automated driving in urban areas and the presented HMI concepts. Reference [91] reported that drivers needed up to three weeks to be used to an ACC system. Thus, the results may change if the participants had already been

