VII. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
The results indicate that the ARHUD supports drivers in partially automated driving better than the Baseline, resulting in shorter take-over times and fewer crashes. This effect is intensiﬁed in the case of the lateral malfunction scenario, which generally appears to be more difﬁcult to control. Furthermore, both HMI concepts showed a high level of attention to the driving environment. Here the ARHUD could have a further advantage, since the participants had a lower attention ratio on the instrument cluster in the vehicle interior. In terms of trust in automation and usability, the ARHUD showed an increase, whereas no signiﬁcant improvement was found for acceptance and workload. However, the high visual attention to the driving environment and the reduction of the take-over time by the ARHUD do not seem to be sufﬁcient for crash avoidance, since the malfunctions were not classiﬁed as controllable in general. A fallback level for partially automated driving by a redundant system, for example an emergency brake assistant, seems to be indispensable in urban areas.
In the future, it should be investigated whether the augmented reality visualization of the trajectory at a larger distance, e.g., 2 seconds in front of the AV, could further improve the controllability by the ARHUD. Furthermore, we suggest that a fallback level should be implemented and its effects on the controllability and subjective rating of the drivers should be investigated. In our investigations we have chosen very timecritical malfunction scenarios in urban areas. To what extent the effects found can be transferred to scenarios with a larger time budget will require a separate investigation.
