Alexander Feierle received the M.Sc. degree in mechatronics and information technology from the Technical University of Munich, Germany, in 2017. He is currently pursuing the Ph.D. degree with the Chair of Ergonomics, Technical University of Munich. He is also working as a Research Associate with the Chair of Ergonomics, Technical University of Munich. His research interests include driver-vehicle interaction and human–machine interface design in the context of automated driving.

Fabian Schlichtherle received the B.Sc. degree in psychology from the Julius-Maximilians-University of Würzburg, Germany, in 2017, and the M.Sc. degree in human factors engineering from the Technical University of Munich, Germany, in 2020. As a Graduate Student, he conducted research at the Chair of Ergonomics, focusing on the beneﬁts of head-up displays in partially automated urban driving.

Klaus Bengler received the Diploma degree from the University of Regensburg, Germany, in 1991, and the Ph.D. degree from the University of Regensburg in cooperation with BMW from the Institute of Psychology (Prof. Zimmer) in 1995 both in psychology. After receiving the Ph.D. degree, he was active on topics of software ergonomics and evaluation of human–machine interfaces. Since May 2009, he has been the Head of the Chair of Ergonomics, Technical University of Munich, Germany, which is active in research areas, such as digital human modeling, human–robot cooperation, driver assistance HMI, and human reliability.

